\begin_body

\begin_layout Section

\lang american
Inequality in a model of capitalist economy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:introduction"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The search of a first-principle model capable of elucidating the emergence and robustness of Pareto's law
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "paretonovo"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is a central challenge in the field of Econophysics.
 Actually,
 as originally reported by Pareto,
 income and wealth real-world present two regimes:
 the upper tail follows a power-law (Pareto's law),
 while the lower values display exponential behavior 
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dragulescu2001,yakovenko2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Such first-principle model would provide a fundamental understanding of the mechanisms governing these properties,
 transcending mere empirical observations to reveal the possible underlying principles that drive economic inequality and its evolution over time.
 In this regard,
 agent-based models
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chakraborti2011"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 particularly kinetic exchange models
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "metzler2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 allow for the simulation of transactions among agents —
such as individuals,
 firms,
 or other economic entities—
 that drive economic dynamics and help identify the underlying processes shaping these systems.
 This approach has paved the way for numerous efforts over the past few decades to explain a variety of phenomena,
 particularly the unequal distribution of wealth (see,
 for instance,
 
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "boghosian2014a,metzler2019,chakraborti2000,dragulescu2000,bouchaud2000"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yakovenko2009,chakraborti2011,chakrabarti2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for reviews).
 It is well known that unbiased random exchanges result in a condensed phase populated by the poorest agents
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "boghosian2014a,Cardoso2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 To prevent such condensation and generate realistic distributions,
 extra mechanisms or regulatory controls have been introduced.
 These mechanisms can include the introduction of specific tax structures
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "LIMA2022,Calvelli2023,Dias2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or other wealth constraints,
 which,
 while effective in shaping outcomes,
 can make the results less purely emergent and more dependent on externally imposed rules.
 Let us give some examples.
 In random asset exchange models,
 the introduction of savings can mitigate condensation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chakraborti2000"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 stabilizing the wealth distribution with a most probable value away from zero.
 The two-regime wealth/income distributions can be reproduced by the introduction of disorder in saving propensity
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Chatterjee2004"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and also by wealth-based advantage mechanisms
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Calvelli2023,Vallejos2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 When networks are considered instead of the more common mean-field setup (where all agents are potentially connected),
 topology can influence the wealth distribution
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "KOHLRAUSCH2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Moreover,
 the power-law exponent can be altered by diverse factors,
 for instance by the distribution of saving propensities
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "AYDINER2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 or by network position exchanges
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "metzler2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In the present paper,
 using agent based simulations,
 we investigate a model that,
 simultaneously gives rise to two key phenomena:
 the emergence of the dual-regime structure in wealth and income distributions,
 alongside the development of a two-class society.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
In his seminal work 
\begin_inset Quotes bls
\end_inset

The Social Architecture of Capitalism
\begin_inset Quotes brs
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wright_2005"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Ian Wright explores the intricate dynamics that results in the self-organization of a capitalist society with a given distribution of wealth among its members.
 Using an agent-based model known as Social Architecture (SA) model,
 Wright provides a theoretical framework aiming to explain the ubiquitous presence of Pareto's law,
 which reflects that a small percentage of a population controls a large portion of a particular resource such as wealth or income.
 Wright's work offers insights into how individual interactions and institutional structures collectively shape economic distributions.
 The model effectively reproduces the so called,
 
\begin_inset Quotes bls
\end_inset

80/20 rule
\begin_inset Quotes brs
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\lang american
The 
\begin_inset Formula $80/20$
\end_inset

 rule derives its name from Pareto's observation of the Italian economy,
 where he found that roughly 20% of the population owned 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim80$
\end_inset

% of the resources.
 The term is used generically,
 although the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $f:(1-f)$
\end_inset

 may vary across different countries and societies,
 reflecting a broader pattern in which a small proportion of inputs or entities accounts for a large share of outcomes or resources
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "boghosian2014,boghosian2014a"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 but,
 most notably,
 it naturally generates the two-pattern shape of the wealth or income distributions (exponential-like,
 for low wealth/income,
 power-law for high wealth/income).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Wright compares his results with real data,
 demonstrating that the model captures a significant portion of the dynamics of the capitalist system.
 In addition to the two regimes of wealth and income distributions,
 the model can reproduce the statistics of other economic indicators,
 such as firm size (power law),
 firm size growth rate (double exponential),
 firm demises per month (log-normal),
 and recession duration (exponential).
 These patterns emerge naturally and robustly from the simple set of rules that define the model.
 Hence,
 the SA model lays the foundation for exploring various economic phenomena,
 starting from microscopic (or agent-based) dynamics controlled by a few parameters.
 Several follow-up studies have been published
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cockshott2012classical,wright2008implicit,LAVICKA2010,ISAAC2019,VILLA2022,MAX2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 but the SA model itself has not been thoroughly analyzed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Our present goal is to investigate aspects related to inequality in income and wealth distributions using the SA model.
 We aim to conduct a comprehensive examination of the role played by the model parameters as well as certain details of Wright's original model.
 Specifically,
 we seek to answer the following questions:
 How do the distributions of income and wealth across different classes depend on parameters such as system size,
 total wealth,
 and average wage?
 How robust are these results?
 Are they universal and,
 if so,
 to what extent?
 Or are they sensitive to key parameters,
 and if so,
 to what degree?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
By analyzing the social and economic structures that contribute to income disparities and wealth concentration in capitalist societies,
 we aim to identify the key parameters and dynamic rules that give rise to Pareto-like distributions and other significant economic measurements.
 Ultimately,
 this paper seeks to contribute to a broader understanding of inequality and concentration phenomena in socio-economic systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The model implemented in this paper is the original SA proposed by Ian Wright
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Wright_2005"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However,
 we have chosen a slightly different notation and terminology to align with those commonly used in econophysics and agent-based modeling.
 The society consists of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 agents (which are labeled 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\dots,N$
\end_inset

 and are not necessarily individuals,
 but can represent other economic entities),
 where each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has a positive integer quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}(t)$
\end_inset

 representing wealth,
 which varies over time.
 Here wealth is defined as an amount of money,
 assets or goods that can be eventually transformed into money,
 owned by each person in a population.
 Although this quantity does not need to be an integer,
 we follow the original proposal,
 as this distinction is not critical to our analysis.
 The size of the population,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 and the total wealth of the system,
 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\sum^{N}_{i=1}w_{i}(t)$
\end_inset

,
 are conserved,
 so the wealth per capita 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}=W/N$
\end_inset

 is constant.
 Agents can be in one of three classes:
 employees (working class),
 employers (capitalist class),
 or unemployed.
 Then,
 each agent is characterized by an index 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\neq i$
\end_inset

,
 which identifies their employer:
 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=j$
\end_inset

 if agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is the employer of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 if agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is unemployed or employer.
 Therefore,
 at any time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
 the state of the entire economy is defined by the set of pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $S(t)=\left\{ \left(w_{i}(t),e_{i}(t)\right):1\leq i\leq N\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 A firm consists of a set of employees and an employer,
 which is the only owner of the firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The model has four parameters:
 system size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 wealth per capita 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}$
\end_inset

,
 and the minimum (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{a}$
\end_inset

) and maximum (
\begin_inset Formula $p_{b}$
\end_inset

) wages employees can receive.
 Unless stated otherwise,
 all random selections (agents,
 wealth aliquots,
 wages,
 etc.) are uniform.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Besides agents (employees and employers),
 the model includes a market value 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

,
 representing goods and services produced by firms.
 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 grows with consumer expenditures and decreases with firm revenues.
 Firms compete for a share of this pool rather than receiving direct payments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
In the initial state,
 all agents have the same wealth (
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}(0)={W}/N$
\end_inset

) and are all unemployed (
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}(0)=0$
\end_inset

).
 At each time step (which we set to be a month as in the original paper),
 the following six steps are executed 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 times,
 to give each agent the opportunity to be active once per month on average.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\lang american
Agent selection
\series default
 – An agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is randomly selected.
 Then,
 the following rules are applied,
 being 3,
 4 and 6 related to wealth exchanges,
 while rules 2 and 5 are related to changes of status.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\lang american
Hiring
\series default
 – Only if 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is unemployed,
 then:
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
A potential employer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is selected from the set 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 of all agents except the employees,
 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(j)=w_{j}/\sum_{n\in H}w_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
If 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\ge\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 (average wage,
 or salary,
 from the distribution of wages),
 then:
 the agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is hired by 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 was unemployed,
 it becomes hence an employer.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\lang american
Expenditure
\series default
 (on goods and services) – A random integer 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,w_{k}\right]$
\end_inset

 is selected for a random agent 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq i$
\end_inset

.
 This amount,
 representing the agent's expenditure,
 is subtracted from their wealth and added to the market value,
 so:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
w_{k} & \to & w_{k}-w,\\
V & \to & V+w.
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\lang american
Market revenue
\series default
 (from sales of goods and services) – Only if 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is not unemployed,
 a random integer 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in[0,V]$
\end_inset

 is transferred from the market value to the agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
 &  & V\to V-w,\\
%{eqnarray*}{eqnarray*}{eqnarray*}{eqnarray*}{eqnarray*}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

In all cases,
 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 counts as revenue for the firm owner,
 either if 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is the owner itself or if 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is an employee (in which case 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the owner).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\lang american
Firing –
\series default
 Only if 
\shape italic
i
\shape default
 is an employer,
 then:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
The number 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 of employees to be fired is defined according to the formula 
\begin_inset Formula $m=max(n_{i}-\frac{w_{i}}{\overline{p}},0)$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the number of agents employed by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
A number 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 of agents from the list of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's employees are randomly selected to be fired.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\lang american
Furthermore,
 if all workers are fired,
 then the employer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 becomes unemployed.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\lang american
Wage payment –
\series default
 Apply only when agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is an employer.
 For each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 employed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 the following transfer occurs 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\to w_{i}-p$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\to w_{j}+p$
\end_inset

,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
where 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is a discrete amount randomly selected from the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[p_{a},p_{b}]$
\end_inset

,
 but if 
\begin_inset Formula $p>w_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 then a new wage 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is selected from the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,w_{i}]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The update is asynchronous,
 that is,
 the system state is updated each time each rule is applied.
 At the end of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 iterations of these six rules,
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is incremented by one unit (i.e.,
 one month).
 Unless otherwise stated,
 the values of the parameters used in the simulations of the model are:
 system size 
\begin_inset Formula $N=1000$
\end_inset

 agents,
 wealth per capita 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}=100$
\end_inset

,
 salary range 
\begin_inset Formula $[p_{a},p_{b}]=[10,90]$
\end_inset

.
 Typically,
 the maximum simulation time is 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{max}=1000$
\end_inset

 years,
 and the values of all state variables are recorded at the end of each year.
 For statistical calculations,
 data were recorded after reaching a steady state,
 discarding a short transient period.
\end_layout

