\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
In the following sections we will take a closer look on this impact of system size and wealth per capita on the economy dynamics.
 We will also see that a key parameter of the model is 
\begin_inset Formula $R\equiv\overline{w}/\overline{p}$
\end_inset

.
 In fact,
 first note that,
 according to the rules,
 the dynamics will not be impacted if wealth is scaled by an arbitrary quantity,
 for example,
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

,
 which only alters the monetary unit.
 Typically,
 we will use 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}=100$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}=50$
\end_inset

,
 unless indicated differently.
 Given the chosen initial state,
 where agents start as unemployed with the same wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}$
\end_inset

,
 then,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R\ge1$
\end_inset

 is necessary to comply with 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{j}\geq\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 (rule 2(b)),
 to start hiring,
 and to prevent the system from becoming trapped in its initial state.
