\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
We analyze various wage ranges,
 
\begin_inset Formula $[p_{a},p_{b}]$
\end_inset

,
 where salaries are uniformly distributed,
 and explore their impact on the distribution of wealth.
 By varying the wage intervals while keeping the mean value 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 fixed,
 we found that the results remain notably invariant to the wage range,
 as shown in Fig.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:wageranges"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

(a).
 This is in agreement with the results for uniform distributions reported in Ref.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "diss_mods_00007721"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The same holds for bimodal distributions,
 as illustrated in the figure for 
\begin_inset Formula $p=10$
\end_inset

 and 90,
 with probability 1/2.
 We also explored non-uniform salary distributions,
 finding again no significant differences.
 Consequently,
 a significant conclusion not addressed in the original model is that the effect of wages is determined by their mean value 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

.
 Furthermore,
 the same results are consistently observed for the same ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $R=\overline{w}/\overline{p}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 decreasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 exerts an identical effect as increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}$
\end_inset

,
 both enhancing inequality.
